<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Civita Castellana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Civita Castellana is a town in the province of Viterbo in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, built on a tufa plateau above deep ravines. It stands on the site of Falerii, the chief town of the Faliscans, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> destroyed in 241 BCE, moving its people to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falerii-novi">Falerii Novi</a> on the plain. The site was resettled in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and has a Romanesque cathedral.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/civita-castellana.jpg" alt="Civita Castellana" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CIVITACASTELLANA at Italian Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Civita_Castellana_(VT)_-_piazza_Giacomo_Matteotti_-_vista_notturna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Civita Castellana is a town and comune in the province of Viterbo, 65 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Mount Soracte lies about 10 kilometres to the south-east. The site was settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> by the Italic Falisci, who called it Falerii.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Faliscan defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, the Romans built a new city about 5 kilometres away, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falerii-novi">Falerii Novi</a>. The abandoned city was repopulated from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and took the name Civita Castellana, meaning City of the Castle, first mentioned in 994. In the following centuries it was a flourishing independent commune, often disputed between the Pope and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. Emperor Otto III died on 23 January 1002 at the castle of Paterno, whose ruins survive.</p><p>Pope Paschal II captured the city at the beginning of the 12th century, and it was later given as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> to the Savelli by Pope Gregory XIV. Pope Sixtus IV assigned it to Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Rodrigo Borgia</a>, the future Pope Alexander VI, who began the Rocca, the citadel, designed by Antonio da Sangallo the Elder. It was completed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> and enlarged under him and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a>.</p><p>The town became an important road hub with its connection to the Via Flaminia in 1606 and with the Ponte Clementino, built after the Battle of Civita Castellana. In that battle, on 5 December 1798, a French army defeated a Neapolitan army while the town was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> of 1798 to 1799.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The cathedral of Santa Maria Maggiore has a Cosmatesque portico erected in 1210 by Laurentius Romanus, his son Jacobus and his grandson Cosmas, with ancient columns and mosaic decoration. Its interior was modernised in the 18th century, and the high altar is made from a Paleo-Christian sarcophagus of the 3rd or 4th century. The church of Santa Chiara has a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> portal of 1529, and the belltower of Santa Maria del Carmine, of the 12th century, incorporates ancient Roman spolia. The National Museum of the Faliscan Countryside houses finds from ancient Falerii and the surrounding area.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Civita Castellana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Falerii, later called Falerii Veteres, stood on a steep tufa plateau at modern <b>Civita Castellana</b> in the Ager Faliscus, between the Tiber and Monte Cimino.</blockquote><a href="/state/falerii">Falerii</a></li></ul></section>
